  "id": "DrugBank_DB00338 - MONDO_6896",
  "verbalization": "Omeprazole is implicated in the pathogenesis of peptic esophagitis through its effects on gastric acid secretion, which is a key mechanism underlying the condition. The evidence suggests that Omeprazole, through its proton pump inhibitor (PPI) action, reduces gastric acid production, thereby alleviating symptoms of peptic esophagitis. This mechanism is corroborated by its use in treating gastric ulcers and gastrin secretion abnormalities, which are both associated with peptic esophagitis. The off-label use of Dexlansoprazole and Vonoprazan, both PPIs, further supports the role of acid suppression in preventing or treating peptic esophagitis. The integrated evidence highlights the central role of acid suppression in the pathophysiology of the condition, with Omeprazole serving as a relevant agent in this context. The observed relationships are consistent with the known pharmacological actions of PPIs, and the evidence does not suggest any conflicting or alternative mechanisms.",
